======================================================================================================================
1.  PROVE THE THEOREM RATHER THAN QUOTE IT -- the coefficient 2/3 is doing all the work
======================================================================================================================
  Deep-MOND AQUAL in spherical symmetry gives g(r) = sqrt(G M(r) a_0)/r exactly.  The virial integral is then
     W = -Integral rho r g 4 pi r^2 dr = -sqrt(G a_0) Integral_0^M sqrt(M') dM' = -(2/3) sqrt(G a_0) M^(3/2),
  independent of the density profile.  In steady state 2K + W = 0, so Sum m <v^2> = 2K = (2/3) sqrt(G a_0 M^3).
  Verifying that numerically on three very different profiles, since a profile-independent claim must be checked:
     uniform sphere    numerical / analytic = 1.000000
     exponential       numerical / analytic = 1.000000
     steep power law   numerical / analytic = 1.000000
  [PASS] A1 the theorem is verified numerically on three profiles that share nothing but their total mass, so the coefficient 2/3 is exact and profile-independent as claimed and is not being taken on trust from a citation   (uniform sphere, exponential and steep power law all reproduce the analytic virial to better than 2 percent)

======================================================================================================================
2.  THE TWO MEASUREMENTS, GALAXY BY GALAXY
======================================================================================================================
  SPARC after the standard quality cuts: 147 galaxies
  DEEP-MOND FILTER: the theorem holds only where the whole system is in the deep-MOND regime, so a galaxy enters
  only if its baryonic acceleration is below a_0 at EVERY measured radius.  That filter is essential and it is the
  main reason the naive whole-sample version of this test is meaningless.
  deep-MOND throughout (g_bar < a_0 at every radius): 88 of 147 galaxies
                                  median a_0           16-84 percent
  ROUTE 1  local relation          9.442e-11   4.21e-11 -  2.01e-10
  ROUTE 2  virial theorem          1.438e-10   6.91e-11 -  2.89e-10
  [PASS] A2 (THE TEST) the two routes to a_0 in the SAME galaxies do not give the same answer.  The local relation and the global virial theorem are both consequences of the same field equation and must agree if that field equation is right, and they disagree by well outside the error on the mean   (the virial route sits +0.180 dex from the local route (a factor 1.51), scatter 0.163 dex on N=88 galaxies, standard error 0.017, so 10.3 sigma)
  [PASS] A3 the disagreement is not a choice of footing: the virial route measures a_0 for itself, so the footing enters only through the deep-MOND selection, and the offset is essentially unchanged   (canonical footing +0.180 dex, alternative footing +0.182 dex)

======================================================================================================================
3.  THE SYSTEMATICS, WHICH IS WHERE THIS DIES IF IT DIES
======================================================================================================================
  A galaxy-scale offset of this size has four candidate causes that are NOT new physics.  Each is checked, and any
  one of them surviving is enough to void the result.
  [FAIL] S1 (systematic: truncation, FLAGGED HERE AND RESOLVED IN 3b) the rotation curves are NOT still accumulating most of their baryonic mass at the last measured point, so truncating the virial integral at the edge of the data is not what produces the offset   (the last two radial bins add a median 16.8% of the total baryonic mass; a truncation large enough to explain a 0.18 dex offset would need roughly 10%)
  (systematic: stellar mass-to-light ratio) both routes depend on it, so it does NOT simply cancel.  Route 2 goes
  as M^-3 through the theorem and as M^2 through the integral; route 1 shifts with g_bar.  Testing directly:
     disc mass-to-light = 0.3:  offset +0.029 dex on N=98
     disc mass-to-light = 0.5:  offset +0.180 dex on N=88
     disc mass-to-light = 0.7:  offset +0.288 dex on N=84
  [FAIL] S2 (systematic: mass-to-light ratio) the offset is NOT an artefact of the assumed stellar mass-to-light ratio: varying it across the full range anyone defends moves the offset by less than the offset itself   (offset ranges +0.029 to +0.288 dex as the disc mass-to-light ratio runs 0.3 to 0.7, against an offset of +0.180 dex)
  [PASS] S3 (systematic: residual Newtonian contamination) the offset does not grow with how close a galaxy comes to the high-acceleration regime, which is what a deep-MOND-validity failure would look like   (offset against peak acceleration: slope +0.136 dex per dex, correlation +0.317; a validity failure would give a strong positive correlation)
  [FAIL] S4 (systematic: geometry, and this one is NOT dismissed) the analytic proof in section 1 is for spherical symmetry.  AQUAL's deep-MOND virial theorem is geometry-independent because it follows from the conformal invariance of the deep-MOND Lagrangian rather than from spherical symmetry, but this script verified it only for spheres, and a disc is not a sphere.  This check is recorded as UNRESOLVED rather than passed, and a flattened numerical AQUAL solve is the work that would settle it   (the numerical verification covers spherical profiles only; the disc case rests on the analytic conformal-invariance argument, which this script does not itself verify.  Until a flattened AQUAL solve is run, a geometry-dependent coefficient of order the offset cannot be excluded)

======================================================================================================================
3b.  CUT THE TWO SYSTEMATICS THAT BITE, AND SEE WHAT SURVIVES
======================================================================================================================
  S1 and S2 each fail on their own, and each is individually large enough to produce the whole offset.  So the raw
  10-sigma number is meaningless as it stands.  This section removes both and reports what is left.
