  [PASS] S5 (the truncation-clean cut) restricting to galaxies whose baryonic mass has genuinely converged inside the measured rotation curve does NOT remove the offset at the standard mass-to-light ratio, so truncation is not the whole story even though it is large enough to be   (M/L=0.15, truncation<0.05: -0.113 +/- 0.027 dex on N=31; M/L=0.2, truncation<0.05: -0.044 +/- 0.024 dex on N=31; M/L=0.25, truncation<0.05: +0.024 +/- 0.020 dex on N=30; M/L=0.3, truncation<0.05: +0.074 +/- 0.021 dex on N=26; M/L=0.4, truncation<0.05: +0.157 +/- 0.025 dex on N=23; M/L=0.5, truncation<0.05: +0.184 +/- 0.029 dex on N=19; M/L=0.6, truncation<0.05: +0.214 +/- 0.026 dex on N=16)
  
  THE USEFUL WAY TO READ THIS.  The statistical precision here (a couple of hundredths of a dex) is an order of
  magnitude better than the systematics, so the comparison is not a null test -- it is a MEASUREMENT of whichever
  input is least well known.  That input is the stellar mass-to-light ratio, and demanding the framework's own
  field equation be self-consistent DETERMINES it, with no freedom left over.
  [PASS] A4 (WHAT THIS ACTUALLY DELIVERS) requiring the framework's local force law and its own exact virial theorem to return the SAME acceleration constant in the same galaxies measures the stellar mass-to-light ratio at 3.6 microns, and the value it demands sits below what stellar population synthesis gives   (the two routes agree at disc mass-to-light = 0.26 (truncation-clean subsample), against the SPARC standard 0.50 and the population-synthesis value 0.50 +/- 0.1 dex (Schombert and McGaugh 2014; McGaugh and Schombert 2014) -- a difference of 0.29 dex)
  ⚠️ AGAINST INTEREST, and it decides how this may be quoted: the offset at the standard mass-to-light ratio is
  10 sigma STATISTICALLY and roughly 1 sigma SYSTEMATICALLY.  The systematic error bar is set by the very quantity
  being measured, so this is NOT yet a tension with stellar population synthesis and must not be quoted as one.
  It is a systematics-limited measurement with a concrete route to becoming decisive.

======================================================================================================================
3c.  THE MOST LIKELY EXPLANATION, QUANTIFIED -- and it is the boring one
======================================================================================================================
  The virial coefficient proved in section 1 is 2/3, derived for SPHERICAL symmetry.  AQUAL's deep-MOND virial
  theorem is argued to be geometry-independent from the conformal invariance of the deep-MOND Lagrangian, but this
  script verified only spheres, and every galaxy in the sample is a DISC.  A flattened system at fixed mass is more
  tightly bound than a sphere, so a coefficient larger than 2/3 is exactly what one would expect.
  Since a_0(virial) goes as the square of the coefficient's inverse, the offset maps onto a required coefficient:
     observed offset +0.180 dex  ->  a disc virial coefficient of 0.820 instead of 0.667,
     which is a 23 percent geometry correction.
  [PASS] A5 (THE HONEST MOST-LIKELY READING) the whole offset is accounted for by a disc virial coefficient about a quarter larger than the spherical one, which is the sign and roughly the size expected for a flattened system, and which this script did not compute.  Until a flattened numerical solve is run, the boring explanation is fully sufficient and must be the default reading   (required coefficient 0.820 against the spherical 0.667, a factor 1.23; a flattened AQUAL solve returning that number closes this entirely, and a flattened solve returning 2/3 turns the same offset into a real problem for either the mass-to-light ratio or the field equation)

======================================================================================================================
4.  mutation controls
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation: pairing each galaxy's virial a_0 with a DIFFERENT galaxy's local a_0 destroys the per-galaxy agreement, so the two routes really are tracking the same object and the comparison is not vacuous   (shuffled pairing scatter 0.571 dex against the matched 0.163 dex)
  [PASS] M2 mutation: the local route recovers the framework's own acceleration constant on the full sample, so route 1 is calibrated and the offset is not route 1 being broken   (route 1 on all 147 galaxies gives a_0 = 9.770e-11, against the canonical footing 9.360e-11)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  Each rotating galaxy yields TWO independent measurements of the acceleration constant: one from the local force
  law at each radius, one from the global virial theorem that the framework's own field equation implies.  A
  modified-GRAVITY theory must satisfy both with the same constant.  A modified-INERTIA theory reproduces the first
  automatically, since it is built from circular orbits where the two arms provably agree, and has no reason to
  satisfy the second.  The theorem is PROVED here rather than cited, and reproduces exactly on three unrelated
  density profiles.  The local route independently recovers the framework's own constant on all 147 galaxies,
  so the machinery is calibrated.
  THE TWO ROUTES DISAGREE BY +0.18 DEX, WHICH IS 10 SIGMA STATISTICALLY AND ABOUT 1 SIGMA SYSTEMATICALLY.
  That is the whole story and the second half is the important half.  Two systematics each individually large
  enough to produce the entire offset failed their checks: rotation-curve truncation (the last two bins carry 17
  percent of the baryonic mass where 10 percent suffices) and the stellar mass-to-light ratio (the offset runs from
  +0.03 to +0.29 dex across the range anyone defends).  A third, the disc geometry, is recorded UNRESOLVED, because
  the proof given here is spherical and a flattened numerical solve was not run.
  SO THIS IS NOT A FORK RESULT AND MUST NOT BE QUOTED AS ONE.  What it is instead is a new and unusually sharp
  MEASUREMENT: the statistical precision is an order of magnitude better than the systematics, so demanding the
  framework be self-consistent between its own force law and its own virial theorem DETERMINES the stellar
  mass-to-light ratio with no freedom.  Tighten the three systematics and the same comparison becomes a genuine
  test of the field equation -- and therefore of which arm is right -- on rotating galaxies alone, where the
  framework is supposed to work and where no dwarf spheroidal sample size can limit it.

